"Plots paired measurements from two test materials for each laboratory to separate shared shifts from inconsistent behavior.\n\nUse when: Several laboratories measure the same two comparable reference materials. Systematic between-laboratory patterns and within-laboratory inconsistency need inspection.\n\nAvoid when: Materials have incomparable scales without an explicit centering or standardization policy.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal position encodes a laboratory’s result on one material. Vertical position encodes its result on the second material, with one point per laboratory.\n\nBuild: Match each laboratory’s paired results and choose comparable axis units. Add reference-center lines using assigned values or a named consensus rule. Use equal aspect when justified and label candidate laboratories outside points.\n\nCheck: Check laboratory identifiers and material pairing carefully. Verify the center and any uncertainty region have a stated calculation. Confirm any diagonal interpretation accounts for material levels and scaling.\n\nPitfalls: Movement along a comparable-scale diagonal may suggest a shared laboratory shift, not prove its cause. An uncertainty circle is meaningful only under its stated variance and independence assumptions.\n\nExample: Compare synthetic measurements of two similar reference solutions across twelve laboratories using assigned centers and labeled unusual pairs.\n\nAlternatives: Bland–Altman plot, Scatterplot, Block plot."
